---
import Head from "../components/head.astro";
import getFalafel from "../lib/falafelLoader";

const falafel = getFalafel();
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
    <Head title="About Duncan’s Wrap Map" />
    <body>
        <main class="text-container">
            <h1>What is this place!?</h1>
            <p>
                This is a list of all the places I’ve eaten falafel wraps. There
                are <strong>{falafel.length}</strong> of them. Just one more and then
                I’ll be happy, right?
            </p>
            <p>
                I’ve eaten a lot of falafel in my time. I’ve done other things,
                too. I’ve picked gooseberries in the summer sun, climbed
                mountains in the driving rain, and seen whales breach the
                surface of the desperate North Atlantic. I’ve laughed, and
                cried, and loved, and lost. I’ve even made websites. But if
                nothing else, I’ve eaten a <strong>lot</strong> of falafel wraps.
                And I’ve enjoyed almost every one.
            </p>
            <p>
                I haven’t been to Lebanon, or Palestine, or Egypt, or Israel, or
                Syria. I probably haven’t had real falafel. Critics will
                denounce me for a falafel map that’s mostly set in England. And
                they’ll be right. I’m a faux-falafel philistine. A Levantine
                lout. I’m eating fake falafel, fib falafel, fraud falafel.
                Pantomime falafel. Costume falafel. I’m watching shadows of
                falafel on the wall of Plato’s cave, and I have the audacity to
                make a website called <a href="/" title="https://falafel.fyi"
                    >https://falafel.fyi</a
                >.
            </p>
            <p>
                But I can’t help where I’ve been. For me, falafel is a thing of
                London. There isn’t much falafel in Milwaukee. When I lived in
                Cornwall, I could rely on pasties for sustenance. But then I
                moved to London, and after a few days, I started getting hungry.
                What would I pick up for after a run? What would I take with me
                on a walk? What would I eat coming home after dancing? Imagine
                the poor vegetarian, adrift in the city. Falafel was a
                revelation.
            </p>
            <p>
                A falafel wrap is kind of the perfect food. It’s hot and cold,
                contained. It’s got all the major food groups. It’s an Egyptian
                food pyramid <span class="kyroh alts">*</span> that can fit in your
                hand.
            </p>
            <p></p>
            <h2>How to use this website</h2>
            <p>
                I’ve had people (friends, even) tell me this map is useless.
                What’s the point of falafel reviews if they’re all Five Stars?
            </p>
            <p>
                That’s the conceit of the map, of course. Every falafel wrap is
                a good falafel wrap.
            </p>
            <p>
                This isn’t a selective list. Some of the falafel here is better,
                and some of it is worse. If I eat a falafel wrap, it goes on the
                falafel map.
            </p>
            <p>
                It’s not a checklist, or a best-of, or a watch mojo top 10. It’s
                a map. If you’re somewhere on Earth and you’re feeling peckish,
                check the map! There might be falafel nearby.
            </p>
            <p>
                You can read the review! If it’s really bad, I probably say
                that. If it’s really good, I probably say that too.
            </p>
            <p>
                Best-of lists are a false promise, I think. A shortcut without a
                view. You think you can just eat the best falafel and be done
                with it? Here’s a horrible fact: most falafel wraps are pretty
                good. If you stick to street vendors, food trucks, and family
                restaurants, chances are you’ll like your falafel. And what if
                you don’t? Who cares? There are joys in every soggy crevice.
                You’ve tried, you’ve learned, you’ve lived.
            </p>
        </main>
    </body>
</html>
